58
00:04:30,450 --> 00:04:32,940
So this is the assignment.

59
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:35,970
This is an assignment that's
used in Stanford where students

60
00:04:35,970 --> 00:04:37,730
actually encode the game.

61
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:41,670
So in this case,
compared to the settings

62
00:04:41,670 --> 00:04:43,860
we're at where we assume
you have the environment

63
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:45,300
and then you're
learning an agent

64
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:47,360
to act in that
environment, here,

65
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:49,050
students are actually
creating the code

66
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:53,190
to make the Breakout assignment,
so to make the game environment.

67
00:04:53,190 --> 00:04:55,800
And this, generally, is
often really engaging and fun

68
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:58,300
for students, particularly when
they're learning to program.

69
00:04:58,300 --> 00:04:59,755
Many people like computer games.

70
00:04:59,755 --> 00:05:02,130
So this is a really great
opportunity for people to learn

71
00:05:02,130 --> 00:05:04,110
and can be really engaging.

72
00:05:04,110 --> 00:05:07,010
And a lot of different people
use these type of assignments.

73
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:09,740
So it's not just at Stanford,
but many, many other places,

74
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:11,860
including code.org
and others use

75
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:14,920
this assignment to try to teach
students about programming.

76
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:17,210
Here's the problem.

77
00:05:17,210 --> 00:05:20,170
Even though it teaches lots of
different introductory computer

78
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:22,790
science concepts,
there's a challenge,

79
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,210
which is, if you want
people to learn from writing

80
00:05:25,210 --> 00:05:27,377
this assignment, you need
to be able to provide them

81
00:05:27,377 --> 00:05:28,040
with feedback.

82
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:30,070
And providing them
with feedback involves

83
00:05:30,070 --> 00:05:31,610
grading the assignments.

84
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:35,110
So in this case, we
normally have a rubric

85
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:36,760
of different things
that the program

86
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:38,630
is expected to do correctly.

87
00:05:38,630 --> 00:05:41,270
Like, is the paddle
drawn correctly?

88
00:05:41,270 --> 00:05:42,610
Is the ball drawn correctly?

89
00:05:42,610 --> 00:05:47,470
When you bounce, does that
respect the desired transition

90
00:05:47,470 --> 00:05:48,110
dynamics?

91
00:05:48,110 --> 00:05:48,982
Things like that.

92
00:05:48,982 --> 00:05:51,190
And so normally, just like
when you guys get feedback

93
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:54,190
from Gradescope, someone has
to go through and play the game

94
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:56,230
to do this.

95
00:05:56,230 --> 00:05:59,320
And so that is really
expensive because that

96
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:03,260
means people have to figure
out, when the ball bounces here,

97
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:05,278
does it actually
do the right thing?

98
00:06:05,278 --> 00:06:06,820
And then you have
to do that for each

99
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:08,470
of the different rubric items.

100
00:06:08,470 --> 00:06:11,110
So there, for
example, it jittered.

101
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:12,790
It didn't do the right thing.

102
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:15,550
So what you can think of here
is that essentially someone is

103
00:06:15,550 --> 00:06:17,930
manually designing
a mental policy,

104
00:06:17,930 --> 00:06:20,140
a grader is designing a
mental policy in their head

105
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:22,270
for how to play
this game in order

106
00:06:22,270 --> 00:06:25,627
to uncover whether the
game dynamics are correct.

107
00:06:25,627 --> 00:06:27,460
And so the way we
normally do that right now

108
00:06:27,460 --> 00:06:30,380
is, each individual grader
figures out how to do that.

109
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:31,992
And then they play this.

110
00:06:31,992 --> 00:06:33,700
So this means that it
would take probably

111
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:36,220
around eight minutes
per submission.

112
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:39,670
So you can't just do a
unit test in the normal way

113
00:06:39,670 --> 00:06:42,040
because you actually
are trying to figure out

114
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,410
how the game behaves
in different scenarios

115
00:06:44,410 --> 00:06:46,690
where it might take
multiple actions to even get

116
00:06:46,690 --> 00:06:49,590
to that scenario.

117
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:52,840
So if you think about doing
eight minutes per submission,

118
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:54,668
if you have 300
submissions in a course

119
00:06:54,668 --> 00:06:56,460
and there's actually
many, many more people

120
00:06:56,460 --> 00:06:59,130
than that have played
this game on code.org,

121
00:06:59,130 --> 00:07:02,020
or tried to code this, that's an
enormous amount of grading time.

122
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:04,388
That's an enormous amount
of human resource time.

123
00:07:04,388 --> 00:07:06,180
And that means that
some of the people that

124
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:10,155
offer this challenge to
students don't grade it at all--

125
00:07:10,155 --> 00:07:11,640
just too expensive.

126
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:13,950
And so that means students
get the opportunity

127
00:07:13,950 --> 00:07:16,270
of trying to do this
exciting assignment,

128
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:18,000
but they don't get
any feedback back,

129
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,900
which can really hinder
their learning process.

130
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:24,900
So there's a lot of things
that make this hard.

131
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:26,650
It's a stochastic setting.

132
00:07:26,650 --> 00:07:29,610
There's not kind of
simple heuristics.

133
00:07:29,610 --> 00:07:32,460
And there are multiple errors.

134
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:36,427
So my student, Chris Pietsch--
another professor here--

135
00:07:36,427 --> 00:07:38,010
and I started think
about this problem

136
00:07:38,010 --> 00:07:39,780
a few years ago of
saying, couldn't we

137
00:07:39,780 --> 00:07:41,580
design like a reinforcement
learning agent

138
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:42,623
to play this game?

139
00:07:42,623 --> 00:07:44,790
And what we want is that
this reinforcement learning

140
00:07:44,790 --> 00:07:46,890
agent can explore the
parts of the domain

141
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:49,270
so that we can try to
uncover how they're doing

142
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:52,480
and whether the game
is coded correctly.

143
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:58,160
So we did this work and then we
did an initial approach to this.

144
00:07:58,160 --> 00:07:59,830
And then Evan, who
is the key author

145
00:07:59,830 --> 00:08:03,710
of this, extended this to try
to think about rubric items.

146
00:08:03,710 --> 00:08:05,800
So the idea is that
instead of having humans

147
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:07,660
graded, what we're
going to do is

148
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:10,630
we're going to replace humans
by a machine learning agent.

149
00:08:10,630 --> 00:08:14,560
And in particular, what
Evan did is he built on our,

150
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:17,560
and I and Chris's initial work
and said, let's actually phrase

151
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:21,760
this as, think about how we
can use meta reinforcement

152
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:23,780
learning and exploration.

153
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:25,900
The reason this is an
exploration problem

154
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:28,990
is because you
want to learn an RL

155
00:08:28,990 --> 00:08:31,630
policy here so that
in a new environment

156
00:08:31,630 --> 00:08:35,890
you can quickly use behaviors
to grade the assignment.

157
00:08:35,890 --> 00:08:38,549
And so that's where efficient
exploration is coming in.

158
00:08:38,549 --> 00:08:40,210
So you don't want
this to have to take

159
00:08:40,210 --> 00:08:42,049
20 minutes to try to grade it.

160
00:08:42,049 --> 00:08:44,179
You want to, as
quickly as possible,

161
00:08:44,179 --> 00:08:47,080
whether for an agent or a human,
figure out what strategy you

162
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:48,700
should use to play
the game in order

163
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:52,900
to correctly grade whether
this is a good environment.

164
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:55,840
And so, Evan had a really
nice NURBS paper building

165
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:57,010
on our NURBS paper.

166
00:08:57,010 --> 00:09:00,412
These are both machine learning
contributions of how to--

167
00:09:00,412 --> 00:09:01,870
there's a series
of papers, there's

168
00:09:01,870 --> 00:09:04,590
a first paper on how we
could do this at all.

169
00:09:04,590 --> 00:09:06,100
There's a second
paper by Evan who

170
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:10,810
is looking at trying to do
explicit exploration, really

171
00:09:10,810 --> 00:09:12,842
fast exploration, and
then we joined forces

172
00:09:12,842 --> 00:09:14,800
to think about how we
could do fast exploration

173
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:15,860
in this setting.

174
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:18,160
And then more recently,
we published a paper

175
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:20,470
showing that this could
actually significantly reduce

176
00:09:20,470 --> 00:09:23,560
grading time and actually
improve accuracy when

177
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:25,210
you combine this with humans.

178
00:09:25,210 --> 00:09:27,040
So, I just give
this as an example

179
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:32,080
to illustrate another exciting
exploration case where

180
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:35,560
if you can design agents
that can learn quickly,

181
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:37,580
and can quickly
explore an environment,

182
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:39,200
it can end up being
really helpful.

183
