241
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:11,783
So that's going to be the key--

242
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,970
we're going to be doing-- this
is an upper confidence bound

243
00:12:18,970 --> 00:12:19,790
algorithm.

244
00:12:22,550 --> 00:12:25,550
So this is going to, again,
use the idea of optimism

245
00:12:25,550 --> 00:12:26,700
under uncertainty.

246
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:29,530
And we're going to think about
how do we bring this to MDPs.

247
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:35,720
So the key idea in this case
is what we would like to do

248
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:38,720
is we'd like to construct
an optimistic upper bound

249
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:40,580
on the Q-function.

250
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:42,585
This is a model-based
approach, which

251
00:12:42,585 --> 00:12:44,210
means the way we're
going to do that is

252
00:12:44,210 --> 00:12:47,330
we're going to try to
construct optimistic estimates

253
00:12:47,330 --> 00:12:49,970
of the reward function,
and optimistic estimates

254
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:51,500
of the dynamics model.

255
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:53,210
It shouldn't be
immediately obvious

256
00:12:53,210 --> 00:12:55,460
what it means to be
optimistic with respect

257
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:59,780
to the dynamics model, and we'll
go through that in a minute.

258
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:02,440
In practice, what we're
going to do is the following.

259
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:04,670
The reward is the
easiest to start with.

260
00:13:04,670 --> 00:13:07,670
So in the reward
case, we're going

261
00:13:07,670 --> 00:13:09,470
to maintain counts
of how many times

262
00:13:09,470 --> 00:13:12,170
we've taken an action
in a particular state.

263
00:13:12,170 --> 00:13:14,470
We're also going to maintain
counts of how many times

264
00:13:14,470 --> 00:13:16,220
we've started in a
state, taken an action,

265
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:18,890
and went to a
particular next state.

266
00:13:18,890 --> 00:13:21,320
And we've seen these ideas
before for tabular Markov

267
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:22,440
decision processes.

268
00:13:22,440 --> 00:13:25,310
We've used them for certainty
equivalent planning back

269
00:13:25,310 --> 00:13:28,160
in the first couple
of weeks of class.

270
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:30,290
So the reward model
is perhaps closest

271
00:13:30,290 --> 00:13:32,097
to what we've seen
for the bandit before.

272
00:13:32,097 --> 00:13:33,680
For the reward model,
what we're going

273
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:37,370
to do is we're going to compute
the empirical average over

274
00:13:37,370 --> 00:13:39,830
in this state and this action,
what's our average reward

275
00:13:39,830 --> 00:13:41,190
we've seen so far.

276
00:13:41,190 --> 00:13:44,090
And then, we're going
to think of there

277
00:13:44,090 --> 00:13:48,308
being an upper
confidence bound to that.

278
00:13:48,308 --> 00:13:50,060
What we're also going
to do in this case

279
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:53,270
is we're going to maintain
an empirical estimate

280
00:13:53,270 --> 00:13:55,640
of the dynamics model.

281
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:58,220
Now, when we do
this, we're going

282
00:13:58,220 --> 00:14:02,600
to do the normal Bellman
equation, except for we're

283
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:06,370
going to include a bonus.

284
00:14:06,370 --> 00:14:09,730
So this part should
look familiar to what

285
00:14:09,730 --> 00:14:15,610
we've seen for Hoeffding, which
is when we are going to compute

286
00:14:15,610 --> 00:14:20,600
a Bellman backup, instead
of uncertainty equivalence,

287
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:23,590
we would just use the empirical
estimate of the reward function

288
00:14:23,590 --> 00:14:26,253
and the empirical estimate
of the dynamics model.

289
00:14:26,253 --> 00:14:27,670
Instead of doing
that, we're going

290
00:14:27,670 --> 00:14:30,430
to include this bonus term.

291
00:14:30,430 --> 00:14:33,780
This is just bonus term.

292
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:38,010
And there's a few
different ways to do

293
00:14:38,010 --> 00:14:39,910
model-based interval estimation.

294
00:14:39,910 --> 00:14:42,310
I'm picking one here that
just uses a bonus term,

295
00:14:42,310 --> 00:14:44,470
but I'll talk about
some other ones.

296
00:14:44,470 --> 00:14:46,380
There's a number of variants.

297
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:51,300
So what this is saying is when
I do my Bellman backup of what

298
00:14:51,300 --> 00:14:54,690
is the expected discounted sum
of rewards from starting state S

299
00:14:54,690 --> 00:14:56,520
and taking action
A, this is going

300
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:58,490
to try to approximate Q star.

301
00:14:58,490 --> 00:15:00,240
I'm going to plug in
my empirical estimate

302
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:01,320
of the reward.

303
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:04,000
I'm going to use my empirical
estimate of the dynamics model,

304
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,930
and then I'm going
to add in a bonus.

305
00:15:06,930 --> 00:15:10,030
And if I have not taken that
state and action very much,

306
00:15:10,030 --> 00:15:11,640
that bonus is going
to be really large

307
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:14,070
because those counts of the
number of states and actions

308
00:15:14,070 --> 00:15:15,990
is going to be really small.

309
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:21,410
So this will be large
if the counts are small.

310
00:15:24,370 --> 00:15:26,920
The key difference compared to
what we've seen with bandits

311
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:29,510
before is this is
a Bellman backup.

312
00:15:29,510 --> 00:15:33,700
So we will then repeat
this many, many times.

313
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:35,960
So you do this for all
states and actions,

314
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:38,360
and then you back up and
you do this many times.

315
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:40,510
So intuitively,
what's happening here

316
00:15:40,510 --> 00:15:43,570
is this is like pretending
the expected discounted

317
00:15:43,570 --> 00:15:45,183
sum of rewards you'd get.

318
00:15:45,183 --> 00:15:46,600
If you start in a
particular state

319
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:49,270
and take a particular
action is much higher

320
00:15:49,270 --> 00:15:52,720
if you have not visited that
state and action very much.

321
00:15:52,720 --> 00:15:55,750
So that's where this
optimism comes in.

322
00:15:55,750 --> 00:15:58,540
You end up adding in
this bonus term here.

323
00:15:58,540 --> 00:16:00,650
And this bonus term
will be really large.

324
00:16:00,650 --> 00:16:02,800
So beta is defined up here.

325
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:06,130
This bonus term, this
is 1 over 1 minus gamma.

326
00:16:06,130 --> 00:16:09,290
So if you imagine that all
rewards are scaled between 0

327
00:16:09,290 --> 00:16:12,770
and 1, and gamma
is really small,

328
00:16:12,770 --> 00:16:14,830
you can think of
that as being like H

329
00:16:14,830 --> 00:16:18,910
times that special term
divided by the square root

330
00:16:18,910 --> 00:16:21,910
of the number of times you've
been in that state in action.

331
00:16:21,910 --> 00:16:24,140
So what that means is
when you do these repeated

332
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:27,080
Bellman backups, it
will drive your policy

333
00:16:27,080 --> 00:16:29,720
to visit parts of the state
and action which you have not

334
00:16:29,720 --> 00:16:31,430
visited much.

335
00:16:31,430 --> 00:16:31,930
OK?

336
00:16:31,930 --> 00:16:33,090
Because those are
the parts where

337
00:16:33,090 --> 00:16:35,430
you're going to have these
really large overestimates,

338
00:16:35,430 --> 00:16:37,918
probably overestimates--
optimistic estimates,

339
00:16:37,918 --> 00:16:38,460
I should say.

340
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:39,918
You have these
optimistic estimates

341
00:16:39,918 --> 00:16:42,540
of how good the value
could be in those states.

342
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:46,200
And the reason this is important
is because it might be--

343
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:47,700
so this is going
to work when you're

344
00:16:47,700 --> 00:16:49,770
doing a series of
episodes, or you're

345
00:16:49,770 --> 00:16:52,590
working in the same MDP
for a long time, what

346
00:16:52,590 --> 00:16:55,950
will happen is that your MDE
will explore in your MDP,

347
00:16:55,950 --> 00:16:59,250
and it will drive you to cover
the state and action space,

348
00:16:59,250 --> 00:17:01,950
if you think that it
might possibly have

349
00:17:01,950 --> 00:17:03,900
good rewards in those places.

350
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:06,369
So this will drive exploration.

351
00:17:06,369 --> 00:17:08,710
And by doing these
repeated backups here,

352
00:17:08,710 --> 00:17:11,730
you're propagating your
optimism under uncertainty

353
00:17:11,730 --> 00:17:14,430
backwards so that you
develop this policy to drive

354
00:17:14,430 --> 00:17:14,990
you that way.

355
00:17:18,509 --> 00:17:21,780
So this is one of the
quintessential algorithms

356
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:28,200
for doing tabular optimism under
uncertainty based planning.

357
00:17:28,200 --> 00:17:30,640
It is also a PAC algorithm.

358
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:32,900
So we talked about
PAC last time.

359
00:17:32,900 --> 00:17:35,720
PAC, it means it's Probably
Approximately Correct.

360
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:38,230
I'll just write that out again,
just to remind ourselves.

361
00:17:38,230 --> 00:17:47,410
Probably approximately correct.

362
00:17:47,410 --> 00:17:49,060
But now, we're
going to talk about,

363
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:50,960
in particular, Markov
decision processes.

364
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:52,930
So we talked about how
an algorithm is probably

365
00:17:52,930 --> 00:17:55,120
approximately correct
if most of the time it

366
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:57,220
makes a decision that
is close to optimal

367
00:17:57,220 --> 00:18:01,510
and only makes mistakes on a
polynomial number of times.

368
00:18:01,510 --> 00:18:03,190
So we talked about
that last time.

369
00:18:03,190 --> 00:18:05,827
We saw that you don't
have to guarantee this.

370
00:18:05,827 --> 00:18:07,160
You could make mistakes forever.

371
00:18:07,160 --> 00:18:09,170
Like, if you're acting
randomly, that's not--

372
00:18:09,170 --> 00:18:13,570
you would continue
to make this forever.

373
00:18:13,570 --> 00:18:15,590
MBIE is a PAC algorithm.

374
00:18:15,590 --> 00:18:22,780
So what it says is
that let's let script

375
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:26,290
a t denote MBIE-EB's
policy at time step t,

376
00:18:26,290 --> 00:18:28,360
and s t denote the
state at time t.

377
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:31,820
With high probability,
the value of the action

378
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:35,380
the algorithm takes is at least
the value of the optimal action

379
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:37,780
for that state minus epsilon.

380
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:41,110
And it's true on all but
a finite number of steps

381
00:18:41,110 --> 00:18:43,720
with high probability.

382
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:47,670
So this is the number of steps.

383
00:18:47,670 --> 00:18:49,620
And the important
thing here is this

384
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:53,290
is a polynomial in the
size of the state space,

385
00:18:53,290 --> 00:18:56,190
the action space, 1 over
epsilon, and 1 over 1

386
00:18:56,190 --> 00:18:58,960
minus gamma.

387
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:02,380
Now, I always encourage
my research students

388
00:19:02,380 --> 00:19:05,680
to plug in for bounds because
theoretical bounds are

389
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:08,080
beautiful, but it's nice
to know whether or not they

390
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:10,330
are at all related to practice.

391
00:19:10,330 --> 00:19:13,300
So, for example, in this case,
you might imagine, let's say,

392
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:19,422
we have s equals
10 and a equals 10.

393
00:19:19,422 --> 00:19:22,490
And you've said
epsilon is equal to 0.1

394
00:19:22,490 --> 00:19:24,990
and gamma is equal to 0.9.

395
00:19:24,990 --> 00:19:25,490
All right.

396
00:19:25,490 --> 00:19:27,240
So let's just work out
what that would be.

397
00:19:27,240 --> 00:19:34,880
That would be roughly 10
to the 3 times 10 to the 9,

398
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:37,840
or 10 to the 12.

399
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:39,250
So that's a lot.

400
00:19:39,250 --> 00:19:41,170
So what that would
say is that we

401
00:19:41,170 --> 00:19:45,460
are sure by using this algorithm
that we will only make mistakes

402
00:19:45,460 --> 00:19:50,620
on this 10 state MDP,
10 to the 12 time steps.

403
00:19:50,620 --> 00:19:52,390
Now, I don't know
about you, but I

404
00:19:52,390 --> 00:19:55,780
would hope that a 10
state GridWorld MDP,

405
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:59,270
that we could learn to act
substantially faster than that.

406
00:19:59,270 --> 00:20:02,760
So I use it to highlight
that these bounds,

407
00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:05,220
while this might officially
say this is a PAC algorithm,

408
00:20:05,220 --> 00:20:09,500
they can be pretty conservative
in how many mistakes

409
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:10,820
you might make.

410
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:12,680
Now, in practice,
often this optimism

411
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:14,820
under uncertainty algorithm
can work very well.

412
00:20:14,820 --> 00:20:17,040
It doesn't say you will make
this number of mistakes,

413
00:20:17,040 --> 00:20:18,970
it just is an upper bound on it.

414
00:20:18,970 --> 00:20:20,720
But it's good to plug
these things in just

415
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:23,750
to see how tight or
not you think it is

416
00:20:23,750 --> 00:20:26,390
relative to real performance.

417
00:20:26,390 --> 00:20:26,940
All right.

418
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:29,955
So this is a PAC
algorithm and the paper

419
00:20:29,955 --> 00:20:31,580
goes through an
interesting proof of it

420
00:20:31,580 --> 00:20:34,170
to show the
different components.

421
00:20:34,170 --> 00:20:37,010
But one of the key ideas is
something called the Simulation

422
00:20:37,010 --> 00:20:37,590
Lemma.

423
00:20:37,590 --> 00:20:39,990
And that I'm going to go
through at least briefly

424
00:20:39,990 --> 00:20:45,230
because the Simulation Lemma
is one of the many core ideas

425
00:20:45,230 --> 00:20:47,990
when we think about doing
efficient exploration.

426
00:20:47,990 --> 00:20:52,310
And the key idea for
the Simulation Lemma

427
00:20:52,310 --> 00:20:56,150
is the idea that we can relate
the accuracy of our models

428
00:20:56,150 --> 00:20:58,970
to the accuracy of our
learned Q-function.

429
00:20:58,970 --> 00:21:00,150
OK, so that's the key idea.

430
00:21:00,150 --> 00:21:04,295
It's going to say,
if we have bounded--

431
00:21:04,295 --> 00:21:05,920
yeah, I guess I'll
just leave it there.

432
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:14,200
So if we just ensure that we
have good predictive models,

433
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:17,160
we can relate our error
and our predictive models

434
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:19,170
back to our value function.

435
00:21:19,170 --> 00:21:22,620
So let's do that
at least, sketch.

436
00:21:22,620 --> 00:21:27,980
So this is going to be for
do for tabular settings.

437
00:21:27,980 --> 00:21:31,260
So we're going to
assume that we're back

438
00:21:31,260 --> 00:21:34,932
in a finite set of states,
finite set of actions.

439
00:21:34,932 --> 00:21:39,150
So this is one proof of
the Simulation Lemma.

440
00:21:39,150 --> 00:21:42,140
We're going to assume that
we have pi as a fixed policy.

441
00:21:45,870 --> 00:21:50,820
And we are going
to assume that we

442
00:21:50,820 --> 00:21:57,810
have a max norm on the reward.

443
00:21:57,810 --> 00:22:01,250
So we're going to assume--
if you remember back,

444
00:22:01,250 --> 00:22:04,490
let me do R1 minus R2-- we're
going to assume that we have

445
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:06,020
two different MDPs.

446
00:22:06,020 --> 00:22:10,640
So MDP 1, and MDP 2.

447
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:13,370
And these might have slightly
different reward functions

448
00:22:13,370 --> 00:22:15,740
and slightly different
dynamics models.

449
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:20,250
So remember that if we
have the infinity norm,

450
00:22:20,250 --> 00:22:21,882
you can express this
as this is going

451
00:22:21,882 --> 00:22:24,090
to be the place where the
two reward functions differ

452
00:22:24,090 --> 00:22:26,705
the most over our
finite state space.

453
00:22:26,705 --> 00:22:28,870
So if one of them
gives the rewards of 1,

454
00:22:28,870 --> 00:22:32,166
2, 7, 3 and the other one
gives the rewards of 2, 6, 1,

455
00:22:32,166 --> 00:22:34,050
7 we would figure
out for which state

456
00:22:34,050 --> 00:22:35,950
do the two rewards
differ the most.

457
00:22:35,950 --> 00:22:38,880
Let's assume that's
upper bounded by alpha.

458
00:22:38,880 --> 00:22:41,190
We're also going to assume
that we have an upper bound

459
00:22:41,190 --> 00:22:43,310
on the dynamics model.

460
00:22:43,310 --> 00:22:47,640
So we're going to assume
that T of s prime given

461
00:22:47,640 --> 00:22:54,550
s a minus T 2 of S prime
given s a is also bounded.

462
00:22:54,550 --> 00:22:57,630
And I'm going to assume
that's bounded by beta.

463
00:22:57,630 --> 00:23:00,400
So that means from the point of
view of your predictive models,

464
00:23:00,400 --> 00:23:02,370
the two MDPs differ.

465
00:23:02,370 --> 00:23:03,875
You can bound the amount.

466
00:23:03,875 --> 00:23:05,500
And we're going to
show if that's true.

467
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:07,050
Then we also are
going to only differ

468
00:23:07,050 --> 00:23:09,570
in their estimated Q-functions
for a particular policy

469
00:23:09,570 --> 00:23:11,730
by a bounded amount.

470
00:23:11,730 --> 00:23:13,290
So what we have is
we want to compare

471
00:23:13,290 --> 00:23:19,960
what is the Q value of
under model 1 for state

472
00:23:19,960 --> 00:23:25,780
s, a and then following
policy pi versus Q pi 2, s, a.

473
00:23:25,780 --> 00:23:27,880
And the reason this is
going to be important

474
00:23:27,880 --> 00:23:30,700
is because in general,
what we're going to have is

475
00:23:30,700 --> 00:23:33,800
the R1 and R2 are going to
correspond to our uncertainty.

476
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:36,570
So if you think back to
the Hoeffding inequality,

477
00:23:36,570 --> 00:23:39,310
as I told you about, we talked
about how our empirical estimate

478
00:23:39,310 --> 00:23:42,370
could differ from the true
estimate by a bounded amount.

479
00:23:42,370 --> 00:23:44,720
So that should make you
think about this part.

480
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:47,020
R1 could be our empirical
estimate of the reward

481
00:23:47,020 --> 00:23:49,540
and R2 could be the
true unknown one.

482
00:23:49,540 --> 00:23:51,310
And Hoeffding can give
you an upper bound

483
00:23:51,310 --> 00:23:53,065
on what that alpha is.

484
00:23:53,065 --> 00:23:55,780
And similarly, we can get a
bound on the dynamics model

485
00:23:55,780 --> 00:23:57,730
error as well.

486
00:23:57,730 --> 00:24:01,120
So the idea will be to say, if
you end up plugging in, say,

487
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:04,060
like an empirical
estimate of the reward

488
00:24:04,060 --> 00:24:06,748
model and the dynamics
model, how far away

489
00:24:06,748 --> 00:24:08,290
could your estimate
of the Q-function

490
00:24:08,290 --> 00:24:11,290
be from if you actually
knew the true reward model

491
00:24:11,290 --> 00:24:13,270
and the true dynamics model?

492
00:24:13,270 --> 00:24:15,820
So that's why we're doing this.

493
00:24:15,820 --> 00:24:18,260
OK, so this is the difference.

494
00:24:18,260 --> 00:24:20,420
Let's just write down
what that will look like.

495
00:24:20,420 --> 00:24:25,550
So this is going to be
R1 of s, a plus gamma,

496
00:24:25,550 --> 00:24:36,440
sum over s prime, T 1, s prime
given s, a, V 1 pi of S prime,

497
