given that Markov
decision process,

743
00:37:31,540 --> 00:37:34,770
then you have to do planning,
like value iteration,

744
00:37:34,770 --> 00:37:38,710
or something like that to
actually get your Q star.

745
00:37:38,710 --> 00:37:42,030
Once you get your
Q star, then you

746
00:37:42,030 --> 00:37:47,750
can select the optimal action
given that computed Q star.

747
00:37:47,750 --> 00:37:50,030
So computationally, it can
involve a lot more work

748
00:37:50,030 --> 00:37:53,230
than what we saw
in the bandit case.

749
00:37:53,230 --> 00:37:54,950
Then the next question
you might have is,

750
00:37:54,950 --> 00:37:56,275
how do we do this sampling?

751
00:38:00,220 --> 00:38:03,240
So, this is the PSRL algorithm.

752
00:38:03,240 --> 00:38:05,930
It was invented by Ian
Osband, Dan Russo, and Ben Van

753
00:38:05,930 --> 00:38:07,770
Roy, who's here at Stanford.

754
00:38:07,770 --> 00:38:10,340
And these guys were here at
Stanford when they invented it.

755
00:38:10,340 --> 00:38:12,800
The idea is as follows--
and I'll talk about sampling

756
00:38:12,800 --> 00:38:15,275
the dynamics model in a second--

757
00:38:15,275 --> 00:38:17,360
there's going to be
a series of episodes.

758
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:20,580
At the very start of an
episode, given your prior,

759
00:38:20,580 --> 00:38:23,660
your current posterior, you are
going to, for every single state

760
00:38:23,660 --> 00:38:27,602
action pair, sample a dynamics
model and sample a reward model.

761
00:38:27,602 --> 00:38:29,060
Given that, you're
going to compute

762
00:38:29,060 --> 00:38:33,010
Q star for your sampled MDP.

763
00:38:33,010 --> 00:38:34,810
Once you have that
sampled MDP, you're

764
00:38:34,810 --> 00:38:38,630
going to act according to that
policy for the entire episode.

765
00:38:38,630 --> 00:38:42,320
So this computes a Q star
for the entire episode.

766
00:38:42,320 --> 00:38:44,390
You're then [INAUDIBLE]
for t equals 1 to h,

767
00:38:44,390 --> 00:38:46,715
you're going to assume
your episodes are finite.

768
00:38:46,715 --> 00:38:48,590
You're going to act
according to your Q star.

769
00:38:48,590 --> 00:38:50,382
You observe your reward
in your next state,

770
00:38:50,382 --> 00:38:53,290
you're going to repeat.

771
00:38:53,290 --> 00:38:55,600
At the end of the
whole episode, you're

772
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:57,970
going to take all of the
data that you just got,

773
00:38:57,970 --> 00:39:01,060
and you're going to
update your posterior.

774
00:39:01,060 --> 00:39:02,870
So for the reward
model, it can be

775
00:39:02,870 --> 00:39:05,470
very similar to what we saw
last time, where you just

776
00:39:05,470 --> 00:39:07,810
update your counts.

777
00:39:07,810 --> 00:39:11,890
For the dynamics model,
it may not be clear

778
00:39:11,890 --> 00:39:13,540
what you would do in that case.

779
00:39:13,540 --> 00:39:16,935
So in this case, what we would
often probably choose to do--

780
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:24,000
all right, let's write up here.

781
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:27,270
So what we would often do
is reduce a Dirichlet model.

782
00:39:31,440 --> 00:39:34,846
A Dirichlet model is a
conjugate prior for multinomial.

783
00:39:38,770 --> 00:39:42,490
OK, multinomials is what we
can use for our normal dynamics

784
00:39:42,490 --> 00:39:44,410
model here because
on multinomial

785
00:39:44,410 --> 00:39:47,290
allows us to express what
is the probability of going

786
00:39:47,290 --> 00:39:50,530
to any of the next states, given
this current state and action.

787
00:39:50,530 --> 00:39:52,690
So in general, we would
have one multinomial

788
00:39:52,690 --> 00:39:55,210
for each state and action pair.

789
00:39:55,210 --> 00:39:57,410
We are now in the
Bayesian setting.

790
00:39:57,410 --> 00:40:06,280
And so now, so we would
have one per s, a pair.

791
00:40:06,280 --> 00:40:09,610
And this specifies our
probability distribution

792
00:40:09,610 --> 00:40:11,860
over all the next states.

793
00:40:11,860 --> 00:40:20,270
That specifies p over s prime
given s and a for all s prime.

794
00:40:20,270 --> 00:40:21,550
And it has to sum up to 1.

795
00:40:21,550 --> 00:40:23,650
That's the multinomial part.

796
00:40:23,650 --> 00:40:25,870
The part where
we're being Bayesian

797
00:40:25,870 --> 00:40:29,420
is we're assuming we don't know
what all these parameters are.

798
00:40:29,420 --> 00:40:32,440
And so, we have a
prior over them.

799
00:40:32,440 --> 00:40:35,570
And the Dirichlet is
a conjugate prior,

800
00:40:35,570 --> 00:40:37,630
which means that if we
start with a Dirichlet

801
00:40:37,630 --> 00:40:40,890
over multinomial parameters,
we observe something.

802
00:40:40,890 --> 00:40:44,180
So let's say we're interested in
understanding what happens when

803
00:40:44,180 --> 00:40:46,640
we're in state 1 and
we take action 1,

804
00:40:46,640 --> 00:40:50,990
and we observe that we
go to s 3 seven times.

805
00:40:50,990 --> 00:40:54,090
And we observe, we go
to s 7, three times.

806
00:40:54,090 --> 00:40:57,745
Well, I'll do two times.

807
00:41:01,430 --> 00:41:04,220
What that means is that at
the end of that episode,

808
00:41:04,220 --> 00:41:07,400
we would use that data
to change our Dirichlet

809
00:41:07,400 --> 00:41:10,970
distribution over those
multinomial parameters.

810
00:41:10,970 --> 00:41:15,027
Very similar to what we saw
for the beta distribution.

811
00:41:15,027 --> 00:41:15,860
And I don't expect--

812
00:41:15,860 --> 00:41:18,150
I mean, it's an
interesting thing to do,

813
00:41:18,150 --> 00:41:20,730
but I don't expect you
to do that in this class.

814
00:41:20,730 --> 00:41:22,950
Some of you might want to
for part of your projects.

815
00:41:22,950 --> 00:41:25,680
But the key idea here
is that it is conjugate.

816
00:41:25,680 --> 00:41:27,980
So it means that the
posteriors you get

817
00:41:27,980 --> 00:41:30,330
are in the same
family as your priors.

818
00:41:30,330 --> 00:41:33,000
And so, you can use this
to sample multinomials.

819
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:36,350
Essentially, it's just
sampling dynamics models.

820
00:41:36,350 --> 00:41:38,960
So we do in this case.

821
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:40,770
And we do this over
and over again.

822
00:41:40,770 --> 00:41:43,100
And the really key
things to notice here

823
00:41:43,100 --> 00:41:49,460
compared to what we were seeing
before is that we have to sample

824
00:41:49,460 --> 00:41:53,510
this entire MDP and we have
to compute its optimal value

825
00:41:53,510 --> 00:41:56,150
before we act.

826
00:41:56,150 --> 00:42:01,620
So we do all of this computation
before the start of an episode.

827
00:42:01,620 --> 00:42:04,090
And you might-- yeah?

828
00:42:04,090 --> 00:42:08,480
I'm a bit confused about
this sampling the MDP,

829
00:42:08,480 --> 00:42:11,510
but the sampling dynamics
and reward models part.

830
00:42:11,510 --> 00:42:13,250
Oh, this is just
explaining that.

831
00:42:13,250 --> 00:42:13,900
OK.

832
00:42:13,900 --> 00:42:15,070
This is like a comment.

833
00:42:15,070 --> 00:42:16,810
You sample the MDP.

834
00:42:16,810 --> 00:42:20,390
What this means is just for
each of the state and actions.

835
00:42:20,390 --> 00:42:20,890
Yeah.

836
00:42:20,890 --> 00:42:22,180
Good clarification question.

837
00:42:22,180 --> 00:42:24,130
To sample an MDP, what
I mean is that you're

838
00:42:24,130 --> 00:42:27,190
going to define the MDP.

839
00:42:27,190 --> 00:42:31,600
So that means we can completely
specify an MDP, given a known

840
00:42:31,600 --> 00:42:35,207
state and action space, and a
discount factor by specifying

841
00:42:35,207 --> 00:42:37,540
a dynamics model for every
single state and action pair,

842
00:42:37,540 --> 00:42:40,120
and specifying
the rewards model.

843
00:42:40,120 --> 00:42:43,750
And then we'll have to
compute the optimal action.

844
00:42:43,750 --> 00:42:46,600
Now, one thing that
you might wonder about

845
00:42:46,600 --> 00:42:51,880
is, is it important or
necessary that we do

846
00:42:51,880 --> 00:42:55,810
all of this once per episode?

847
00:42:55,810 --> 00:42:59,440
So when we talked
about Bayesian bandits,

848
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:03,100
after every single observation,
we updated our posterior.

849
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:07,040
So we would try buddy
taping the toes,

850
00:43:07,040 --> 00:43:08,930
and we'd see that that
helps someone recover,

851
00:43:08,930 --> 00:43:10,880
and then we would
update our prior.

852
00:43:10,880 --> 00:43:12,500
This is a bit different.

853
00:43:12,500 --> 00:43:16,650
We are only doing
this every h steps.

854
00:43:16,650 --> 00:43:19,590
Now, you might think maybe
that's computational.

855
00:43:19,590 --> 00:43:22,960
You might think that that's
being done for another reason.

856
00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:26,400
But it's something that's
an interesting thing

857
00:43:26,400 --> 00:43:27,070
to think about.

858
00:43:27,070 --> 00:43:28,560
Let me see if I have
this on the next slide.

859
00:43:28,560 --> 00:43:28,740
Yeah.

860
00:43:28,740 --> 00:43:29,910
So let's do a check
your understanding

861
00:43:29,910 --> 00:43:31,785
and then I'll give you
talk a little bit more

862
00:43:31,785 --> 00:43:35,617
about why this is done in PSRL.

863
00:43:35,617 --> 00:43:37,200
So this asks you to
think a little bit

864
00:43:37,200 --> 00:43:39,360
about doing strategic
exploration and MDPs

865
00:43:39,360 --> 00:43:41,705
and in Thompson sampling in
the algorithm I just showed.

866
00:43:50,489 --> 00:43:54,465
[AUDIO OUT]

867
00:44:36,300 --> 00:44:36,820
All right.

868
00:44:36,820 --> 00:44:39,070
I want you compare your
answers with someone near you.

869
00:44:43,580 --> 00:44:47,052
[AUDIO OUT]

870
00:45:42,670 --> 00:45:44,200
OK, thank you-- yeah.

871
00:45:44,200 --> 00:45:45,940
So now it should be back on.

872
00:45:45,940 --> 00:45:50,390
What I was saying is that in
Maria Dimakopoulou's work,

873
00:45:50,390 --> 00:45:52,670
she was thinking about
concurrent reinforcement

874
00:45:52,670 --> 00:45:54,920
learning, which is something
we've also thought about.

875
00:45:54,920 --> 00:45:57,290
And for this much more
realistic setting,

876
00:45:57,290 --> 00:46:00,820
the idea is whether you might
need to coordinate exploration

877
00:46:00,820 --> 00:46:03,700
and how frequently you update.

878
00:46:03,700 --> 00:46:07,280
Now, one of the challenges
of this setting,

879
00:46:07,280 --> 00:46:10,660
even before you get into
concurrent reinforcement

880
00:46:10,660 --> 00:46:15,740
learning, is that if you update
your prior a lot within a task,

881
00:46:15,740 --> 00:46:17,560
like within a single
episode, you're

882
00:46:17,560 --> 00:46:21,160
essentially sampling different
MDPs within the same episode.

883
00:46:21,160 --> 00:46:23,350
The reason that can
be bad is that now

884
00:46:23,350 --> 00:46:25,880
is going to totally
change your behavior,

885
00:46:25,880 --> 00:46:29,560
and there may be some cases
where you essentially thrash.

886
00:46:29,560 --> 00:46:32,890
Let me give an example.

887
00:46:32,890 --> 00:46:36,370
So one of the canonical
hard Markov decision

888
00:46:36,370 --> 00:46:40,730
processes that people
talk about is a chain.

889
00:46:40,730 --> 00:46:42,690
It's really just an
illustrative one.

890
00:46:46,300 --> 00:46:48,885
And there are lots of different
slight variants of chains.

891
00:46:51,780 --> 00:46:56,260
And the idea is that you
might have something--

892
00:46:56,260 --> 00:47:00,370
I've shown stuff that's similar
to this before where on one side

893
00:47:00,370 --> 00:47:01,560
or on the other side--

894
00:47:01,560 --> 00:47:04,700
oh, it's not reconnecting
just in the back there,

895
00:47:04,700 --> 00:47:06,910
you need to reconnect.

896
00:47:06,910 --> 00:47:11,020
That you would have high reward
on one or the other sides.

897
00:47:11,020 --> 00:47:12,680
What you could
imagine in this case,

898
00:47:12,680 --> 00:47:15,350
if you were thinking about it
being like a Bayesian bandit,

899
00:47:15,350 --> 00:47:17,920
is that some of the times, it
might pick a Markov decision

900
00:47:17,920 --> 00:47:21,370
process where this is the
good, the best state, and some

901
00:47:21,370 --> 00:47:23,965
of the time, it might pick
a Markov decision process--

902
00:47:23,965 --> 00:47:28,930
oh it's still not
showing on there.

903
00:47:28,930 --> 00:47:29,870
Thanks.

904
00:47:29,870 --> 00:47:31,810
Hopefully that will come up.

905
00:47:31,810 --> 00:47:35,040
And some of the time it
might pick one that is here.

906
00:47:35,040 --> 00:47:39,730
So if you start off acting,
let's say that you first sampled

907
00:47:39,730 --> 00:47:43,570
an MDP where this is the best
state, you do your planning

908
00:47:43,570 --> 00:47:46,120
and then your agent is going
to start going this way.

909
00:47:46,120 --> 00:47:47,110
OK?

910
00:47:47,110 --> 00:47:48,610
Let's say you
observe that there's

911
00:47:48,610 --> 00:47:51,700
some zero reward here and your
Thompson sampling updates.

912
00:47:51,700 --> 00:47:56,452
And now, it says, hey,
this is the best state

913
00:47:56,452 --> 00:47:58,910
because you just have some
prior over the model parameters.

914
00:47:58,910 --> 00:48:00,952
And so your agent turns
around and it's like, oh,

915
00:48:00,952 --> 00:48:04,127
I shouldn't go this way,
I should go this way.

916
00:48:04,127 --> 00:48:06,460
And then as you're doing that,
it's getting more rewards

917
00:48:06,460 --> 00:48:08,172
and it's updating its posterior.

918
00:48:08,172 --> 00:48:10,755
And so then, it samples again
and it's like, oh, this is good.

919
00:48:14,680 --> 00:48:17,830
And so, it can lead to this
kind of thrashing behavior

920
00:48:17,830 --> 00:48:20,920
because it's sampling
a new Markov decision

921
00:48:20,920 --> 00:48:22,040
process each time.

922
00:48:22,040 --> 00:48:24,970
And so your agent can end
up toggling back and forth

923
00:48:24,970 --> 00:48:28,600
between its ideas over
which MDP it's in.

924
00:48:28,600 --> 00:48:31,750
So it's for this
reason that often you

925
00:48:31,750 --> 00:48:35,200
will want to essentially commit
to the Markov decision process

926
00:48:35,200 --> 00:48:37,645
you're in for the whole time.

927
00:48:37,645 --> 00:48:39,020
You don't always
have to do this,

928
00:48:39,020 --> 00:48:41,530
but that's one of the reasons
why this can be helpful.

929
00:48:41,530 --> 00:48:47,110
This commitment is also in
the 2013 NURBS paper that--

930
00:48:47,110 --> 00:48:49,470
well, not-- the algorithm
that we saw earlier, right?

931
00:48:49,470 --> 00:48:50,365
They both commit?

932
00:48:52,420 --> 00:48:54,685
Yes.

933
00:48:54,685 --> 00:48:56,560
So far, sorry-- This is
just exactly the same

934
00:48:56,560 --> 00:48:57,533
as the PSRL algorithm.

935
00:48:57,533 --> 00:48:59,450
I'm about to tell you
about the seed sampling.

936
00:48:59,450 --> 00:49:01,550
But yes, this is
just in the 2013.

937
00:49:01,550 --> 00:49:02,480
So yeah, exactly.

938
00:49:02,480 --> 00:49:05,030
This is the in PSRL itself.

939
00:49:05,030 --> 00:49:05,590
It commits.

940
00:49:05,590 --> 00:49:07,280
And this is one of
the reasons for that.

941
00:49:07,280 --> 00:49:09,400
And so, in Maria's
work, she discusses

942
00:49:09,400 --> 00:49:11,108
some of the important
benefits of it.

943
00:49:11,108 --> 00:49:13,150
And then, she thinks about
how would you actually

944
00:49:13,150 --> 00:49:16,120
maybe try to couple and
coordinate exploration

945
00:49:16,120 --> 00:49:18,520
if you have many
agents that are going

946
00:49:18,520 --> 00:49:20,690
through the same
environment at once.

947
00:49:20,690 --> 00:49:24,520
And it's in some ways, it
relates to this idea, too.

948
00:49:24,520 --> 00:49:27,242
You might want everybody
to commit to exploring

949
00:49:27,242 --> 00:49:28,450
different parts of the space.

950
00:49:28,450 --> 00:49:30,710
Because if you have many
agents in the same domain,

951
00:49:30,710 --> 00:49:31,900
you might want to
say, you're going

952
00:49:31,900 --> 00:49:33,480
to think that the
best reward is here.

953
00:49:33,480 --> 00:49:35,397
You're going to think
the best reward is here.

954
00:49:35,397 --> 00:49:38,650
Go explore, and then we'll unify
in our posterior afterwards.

955
00:49:38,650 --> 00:49:42,060
So she has a nice
demonstration of that.

956
00:49:42,060 --> 00:49:44,180
And then she extended it
to the deep learning case

957
00:49:44,180 --> 00:49:45,000
shortly afterwards.

958
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:48,199
But maybe if I can play that.

959
00:49:48,199 --> 00:49:51,692
