972
00:52:25,410 --> 00:52:27,883
OK, so seed sampling
in her case is

973
00:52:27,883 --> 00:52:29,550
what they're doing
when they essentially

974
00:52:29,550 --> 00:52:31,295
do concurrent
reinforcement learning.

975
00:52:31,295 --> 00:52:32,670
And you might have
even missed it

976
00:52:32,670 --> 00:52:34,920
because that part
is really fast.

977
00:52:34,920 --> 00:52:37,230
OK, so I'll move it to this,
just so I can talk over it

978
00:52:37,230 --> 00:52:38,560
at the same time.

979
00:52:38,560 --> 00:52:41,470
So this is seed sampling,
which is what their idea was.

980
00:52:41,470 --> 00:52:44,370
And this just talks again
about doing strategic,

981
00:52:44,370 --> 00:52:45,960
coordinated sampling.

982
00:52:45,960 --> 00:52:48,270
So you can see in this case,
we're leveraging the fact

983
00:52:48,270 --> 00:52:49,853
that you've got
concurrent agents that

984
00:52:49,853 --> 00:52:52,330
are exploring the environment,
they're committing to it,

985
00:52:52,330 --> 00:52:55,090
but they're committing to it in
a way that they coordinate that.

986
00:52:55,090 --> 00:52:56,757
So that you don't get
all of the agents.

987
00:52:56,757 --> 00:52:59,057
So here, by 324,
all of the agents

988
00:52:59,057 --> 00:53:01,140
have shared information
about where the cheese is,

989
00:53:01,140 --> 00:53:04,250
and everyone's solved.

990
00:53:04,250 --> 00:53:05,110
All right.

991
00:53:05,110 --> 00:53:08,160
So that just illustrates
why you both need

992
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:11,650
this committing to a particular
exploration strategy.

993
00:53:11,650 --> 00:53:13,740
And then if you're in
the case where you also

994
00:53:13,740 --> 00:53:16,530
have concurrent agents-- which
is very realistic-- that having

995
00:53:16,530 --> 00:53:19,580
this additional coordination
is really helpful.

996
00:53:19,580 --> 00:53:21,330
Now, I think one of
the interesting things

997
00:53:21,330 --> 00:53:26,700
to note there is that this is a
nice place where there's some--

998
00:53:26,700 --> 00:53:28,050
is it connecting?

999
00:53:28,050 --> 00:53:29,550
Hopefully it'll
connect in a second.

1000
00:53:29,550 --> 00:53:31,770
There's an
interesting disconnect

1001
00:53:31,770 --> 00:53:33,900
between theory and experiment.

1002
00:53:33,900 --> 00:53:35,382
It's still not?

1003
00:53:35,382 --> 00:53:37,090
Maybe there's a problem
with a connector.

1004
00:53:37,090 --> 00:53:40,095
We'll try to get that
fixed for next week.

1005
00:53:40,095 --> 00:53:42,930
There's a disconnect
between theory and practice

1006
00:53:42,930 --> 00:53:46,870
because theoretically, you don't
need to do this exploration.

1007
00:53:46,870 --> 00:53:48,840
So we have a paper
from 2015 showing

1008
00:53:48,840 --> 00:53:50,890
that if you don't do
coordinated exploration,

1009
00:53:50,890 --> 00:53:52,560
it's still totally sufficient.

1010
00:53:52,560 --> 00:53:56,470
You can still get basically
almost a linear speed up.

1011
00:53:56,470 --> 00:53:58,210
Oh good, finally came.

1012
00:53:58,210 --> 00:53:59,280
Yay!

1013
00:53:59,280 --> 00:53:59,860
All right.

1014
00:53:59,860 --> 00:54:05,590
So that covers how you can
do Bayesian exploration

1015
00:54:05,590 --> 00:54:10,240
and optimism under uncertainty
in the tabular Markov decision

1016
00:54:10,240 --> 00:54:11,360
process case.

1017
00:54:11,360 --> 00:54:12,610
But of course, what we'd
like to be able to do

1018
00:54:12,610 --> 00:54:14,320
is to do this for
much more large state

1019
00:54:14,320 --> 00:54:16,360
spaces and realistic problems.

1020
00:54:16,360 --> 00:54:19,600
So this is very much
an ongoing area.

1021
00:54:19,600 --> 00:54:21,610
Again, you'll see
this similarity

1022
00:54:21,610 --> 00:54:23,390
to the types of ideas
we've seen before.

1023
00:54:23,390 --> 00:54:25,840
Very popular ideas are
optimism under uncertainty

1024
00:54:25,840 --> 00:54:26,920
and Thompson sampling.

1025
00:54:26,920 --> 00:54:28,878
They're not the only
ones, but they're probably

1026
00:54:28,878 --> 00:54:31,210
the dominant strategies
people try to use.

1027
00:54:31,210 --> 00:54:35,240
For-- I may have just not
caught this, but specifically,

1028
00:54:35,240 --> 00:54:37,600
what is actually different
between the two algorithms?

1029
00:54:37,600 --> 00:54:42,520
What is the difference in this
sampling, between 2013 and 2018?

1030
00:54:42,520 --> 00:54:43,180
Yes.

1031
00:54:43,180 --> 00:54:44,180
So two things.

1032
00:54:44,180 --> 00:54:47,860
One is that the PSRL does
not think about concurrency.

1033
00:54:47,860 --> 00:54:49,760
So they just assume
there's a single MDP.

1034
00:54:49,760 --> 00:54:51,080
You have a single agent in it.

1035
00:54:51,080 --> 00:54:55,390
The other case assumes you have
m agents all in the same MDP.

1036
00:54:55,390 --> 00:54:57,667
So like the mice trying
to find the cheese,

1037
00:54:57,667 --> 00:54:59,750
there's not just one mouse,
there's a whole bunch.

1038
00:54:59,750 --> 00:55:01,930
And the idea was
seed sampling is also

1039
00:55:01,930 --> 00:55:04,270
to think about how
do you choose which

1040
00:55:04,270 --> 00:55:09,760
MDP they each think they're in
to distribute the exploration.

1041
00:55:09,760 --> 00:55:10,750
OK.

1042
00:55:10,750 --> 00:55:12,873
Yeah.

1043
00:55:12,873 --> 00:55:14,290
And the other case,
you don't have

1044
00:55:14,290 --> 00:55:16,540
to do any coordination because
there's only one agent.

1045
00:55:18,670 --> 00:55:19,340
OK, good.

1046
00:55:19,340 --> 00:55:21,590
So in terms of generalization,
we're going to think about this.

1047
00:55:21,590 --> 00:55:22,990
The reason why this
starts to get more tricky

1048
00:55:22,990 --> 00:55:24,020
is a couple of things.

1049
00:55:24,020 --> 00:55:26,180
One is that for optimism
under uncertainty,

1050
00:55:26,180 --> 00:55:28,340
it means we have to have
a notion of uncertainty.

1051
00:55:28,340 --> 00:55:30,757
And it just gets much harder
to represent uncertainty when

1052
00:55:30,757 --> 00:55:32,560
we have deep neural networks.

1053
00:55:32,560 --> 00:55:34,572
Similarly for Thompson
sampling, as we

1054
00:55:34,572 --> 00:55:36,530
start to move up to really
complicated domains,

1055
00:55:36,530 --> 00:55:38,770
we need posteriors over
really complicated settings

1056
00:55:38,770 --> 00:55:41,230
and that's also computationally
challenging and hard

1057
00:55:41,230 --> 00:55:44,050
to approximate.

1058
00:55:44,050 --> 00:55:47,193
So let's first start
with contextual bandits.

1059
00:55:47,193 --> 00:55:48,610
And some of you
guys will probably

1060
00:55:48,610 --> 00:55:50,840
be doing some of this
for your project.

1061
00:55:50,840 --> 00:55:53,320
So instead of having
our multi-armed bandit,

1062
00:55:53,320 --> 00:55:55,780
now we're halfway
between a Markov decision

1063
00:55:55,780 --> 00:55:57,290
process and a bandit.

1064
00:55:57,290 --> 00:55:59,950
So we're going to assume we have
states, but the action we take

1065
00:55:59,950 --> 00:56:02,050
doesn't influence
the next state.

1066
00:56:02,050 --> 00:56:04,100
And so now, if we
think about rewards,

1067
00:56:04,100 --> 00:56:07,690
we'll have a reward
per action in state.

1068
00:56:07,690 --> 00:56:10,160
And just like what
we've often done before,

1069
00:56:10,160 --> 00:56:12,350
if we have a really large
state and action space,

1070
00:56:12,350 --> 00:56:13,725
we're going to
assume that we use

1071
00:56:13,725 --> 00:56:16,060
some parametric
representation to model

1072
00:56:16,060 --> 00:56:20,460
the relationship between state
and action and output rewards.

1073
00:56:20,460 --> 00:56:22,950
Perhaps not surprisingly,
there is an enormous benefit

1074
00:56:22,950 --> 00:56:23,770
of doing this.

1075
00:56:23,770 --> 00:56:26,850
So if you think
about a setting where

1076
00:56:26,850 --> 00:56:29,520
this is the number
of arms you have,

1077
00:56:29,520 --> 00:56:31,410
if you did something
like upper confidence

1078
00:56:31,410 --> 00:56:32,740
bounds-- and this is a regret.

1079
00:56:32,740 --> 00:56:34,070
Regret is on the y-axis.

1080
00:56:34,070 --> 00:56:36,570
So if you did something like
upper confidence bounds and you

1081
00:56:36,570 --> 00:56:39,280
have 1,000 arms
and 4,000 pulls--

1082
00:56:39,280 --> 00:56:42,900
sorry, you have 1,000 arms and
then you're pulling these over

1083
00:56:42,900 --> 00:56:43,473
time.

1084
00:56:43,473 --> 00:56:45,390
So this is, I think,
just regret after a fixed

1085
00:56:45,390 --> 00:56:46,720
number of time steps.

1086
00:56:46,720 --> 00:56:49,060
Unsurprisingly, if you have
a lot more arms to pull,

1087
00:56:49,060 --> 00:56:51,030
you'll have a lot more regret.

1088
00:56:51,030 --> 00:56:53,880
Because in upper confidence
bounds, in the things

1089
00:56:53,880 --> 00:56:56,820
we've seen so far, you
don't share any information

1090
00:56:56,820 --> 00:56:59,370
across the arms.

1091
00:56:59,370 --> 00:57:01,170
If, on the other
hand, use something

1092
00:57:01,170 --> 00:57:05,430
like linear UCB, which assumes
that your arms are represented

1093
00:57:05,430 --> 00:57:10,380
by a set of features-- so
showing someone like a Trump

1094
00:57:10,380 --> 00:57:13,350
campaign today and a different
Trump campaign tomorrow

1095
00:57:13,350 --> 00:57:14,940
might have the same effect.

1096
00:57:14,940 --> 00:57:17,190
Because they're going to
have a shared set of features

1097
00:57:17,190 --> 00:57:20,140
about Trump, at least would be
one thing that would overlap.

1098
00:57:20,140 --> 00:57:22,120
You can leverage that structure.

1099
00:57:22,120 --> 00:57:23,770
And so, what you
can see in this case

1100
00:57:23,770 --> 00:57:27,280
is that if you leverage say, a
parametric linear representation

1101
00:57:27,280 --> 00:57:31,670
in this case, even as you scale
up the actual number of arms,

1102
00:57:31,670 --> 00:57:34,010
if your parameter space
is still the same,

1103
00:57:34,010 --> 00:57:37,630
then your regret
doesn't scale badly.

1104
00:57:37,630 --> 00:57:42,280
So for example, this is
k, but you know your theta

1105
00:57:42,280 --> 00:57:44,360
in this case.

1106
00:57:44,360 --> 00:57:49,550
The-- Your theta in this case
might just be low dimensional.

1107
00:57:49,550 --> 00:57:55,300
So we might have a
theta, which is in R d,

1108
00:57:55,300 --> 00:57:58,490
so we have a d-dimensional
representation.

1109
00:57:58,490 --> 00:58:01,270
And this just shows that
this can be really helpful.

1110
00:58:01,270 --> 00:58:04,330
In general, you want
to leverage structure.

1111
00:58:04,330 --> 00:58:07,510
So one common thing to
do is to model the reward

1112
00:58:07,510 --> 00:58:09,640
as a linear function.

1113
00:58:09,640 --> 00:58:12,802
Of course, this could be built
on top of a deep neural network,

1114
00:58:12,802 --> 00:58:15,260
or on top of a large language
model or something like that.

1115
00:58:15,260 --> 00:58:16,930
You can often just use
some really complicated

1116
00:58:16,930 --> 00:58:19,030
representation of the
state and action space.

1117
00:58:19,030 --> 00:58:22,020
And then, say, for
the last layer,

1118
00:58:22,020 --> 00:58:23,770
my actual reward is
going to be a function

1119
00:58:23,770 --> 00:58:27,850
of these complicated features,
dot product with some theta

1120
00:58:27,850 --> 00:58:29,925
parameter.

1121
00:58:29,925 --> 00:58:31,300
And one common
thing is to assume

1122
00:58:31,300 --> 00:58:34,120
that it's just a linear
function plus some noise.

1123
00:58:34,120 --> 00:58:36,710
And the nice thing about this
is that if your features are

1124
00:58:36,710 --> 00:58:38,710
interpretable, then your
reward function is also

1125
00:58:38,710 --> 00:58:40,335
very interpretable
because you can just

1126
00:58:40,335 --> 00:58:42,940
think of relatively, how much
do each of those features

1127
00:58:42,940 --> 00:58:44,100
contribute to your reward?

1128
00:58:46,910 --> 00:58:48,560
All right.

1129
00:58:48,560 --> 00:58:54,455
So one thing to think about in
this case is in these settings--

1130
00:58:54,455 --> 00:58:56,330
well, I'll go a little
fast through this part

1131
00:58:56,330 --> 00:58:59,280
because I want to make sure
we get to the MDP part two.

1132
00:58:59,280 --> 00:59:04,740
But when you have this, even if
you have a linear set of models,

1133
00:59:04,740 --> 00:59:08,570
you can use them to represent
more complicated functions.

1134
00:59:08,570 --> 00:59:14,430
Because, let's say--
technology is getting--

1135
00:59:14,430 --> 00:59:18,740
So let's say this is your
reward model for three

1136
00:59:18,740 --> 00:59:19,620
different actions.

1137
00:59:19,620 --> 00:59:23,900
This is a 1, this is
a 2, and this is a 3.

1138
00:59:23,900 --> 00:59:26,000
And this is what your reward is.

1139
00:59:26,000 --> 00:59:27,900
And this is your
state and space.

1140
00:59:27,900 --> 00:59:31,110
So let's imagine that you
had a linear representation.

1141
00:59:31,110 --> 00:59:33,920
Then, you could
represent policies

1142
00:59:33,920 --> 00:59:36,680
that are just joint linear
because if you were taking

1143
00:59:36,680 --> 00:59:40,730
the max here, this is what the
value would be of your policy.

1144
00:59:40,730 --> 00:59:43,010
Because it would say, a
1 dominates for this part

1145
00:59:43,010 --> 00:59:44,540
of the state space.

1146
00:59:44,540 --> 00:59:46,680
A 3 dominates for this
part of the state space,

1147
00:59:46,680 --> 00:59:49,500
and a 2 dominates for this
part of the state space.

1148
00:59:49,500 --> 00:59:51,832
So linear ones--

1149
00:59:51,832 --> 00:59:53,790
I guess the main point
here is that even if you

1150
00:59:53,790 --> 00:59:56,580
have a linear reward
model, it doesn't mean

1151
00:59:56,580 --> 00:59:58,300
your policy has to be linear.

1152
00:59:58,300 --> 01:00:00,490
Your policy will
be disjoint linear.

1153
01:00:00,490 --> 01:00:03,780
It can be made up of
these sorts of functions.

1154
01:00:03,780 --> 01:00:05,890
So it's fairly flexible.

1155
01:00:05,890 --> 01:00:07,862
OK, how would we
work in these cases?

1156
01:00:07,862 --> 01:00:10,070
Well, in this case, what it
means to have uncertainty

1157
01:00:10,070 --> 01:00:14,010
is we need to have uncertainty
over this linear vector.

1158
01:00:14,010 --> 01:00:17,390
So, we want to capture
uncertainty over theta

1159
01:00:17,390 --> 01:00:19,983
through some sort
of uncertainty set.

1160
01:00:19,983 --> 01:00:21,650
And there's been a
lot of beautiful work

1161
01:00:21,650 --> 01:00:24,380
to try to quantify the
types of uncertainties

1162
01:00:24,380 --> 01:00:28,610
we have through things like
the elliptical potential lemon,

1163
01:00:28,610 --> 01:00:30,530
things like that, which
give us, basically,

1164
01:00:30,530 --> 01:00:33,840
just sort of an uncertainty
set over vectors.

1165
01:00:33,840 --> 01:00:37,640
And you can do this in a
computationally tractable way.

1166
01:00:37,640 --> 01:00:39,890
And what this means is it
gives us a principled way

1167
01:00:39,890 --> 01:00:42,710
to get an upper confidence bound
on the reward function, given

1168
01:00:42,710 --> 01:00:45,170
that we have uncertainty
over linear model.

1169
01:00:45,170 --> 01:00:48,290
And this was shown to be
very useful for news article

1170
01:00:48,290 --> 01:00:50,960
recommendations
about 14 years ago.

1171
01:00:50,960 --> 01:00:53,570
And you can also
look at chapter 19.

1172
01:00:53,570 --> 01:00:55,670
So these are really useful.

1173
01:00:55,670 --> 01:00:59,870
This is one way to represent
a contextual bandit setting

1174
01:00:59,870 --> 01:01:03,380
when you want to
handle generalization.

1175
01:01:03,380 --> 01:01:05,700
We'll now talk briefly
about how you might do this

1176
01:01:05,700 --> 01:01:08,400
for Markov decision processes.

1177
01:01:08,400 --> 01:01:11,580
OK, so if we think back
to the MBIE-EB algorithm

1178
01:01:11,580 --> 01:01:16,756
for finite state and actions,
we have to modify a few things.

1179
01:01:16,756 --> 01:01:22,380
So if we think about this, we
were keeping track of counts.

1180
01:01:22,380 --> 01:01:24,280
And we were doing this--

1181
01:01:24,280 --> 01:01:26,220
we were building
a model separately

1182
01:01:26,220 --> 01:01:28,260
for every state and action.

1183
01:01:28,260 --> 01:01:33,203
So this count-based term here
that we're using as a bonus,

1184
01:01:33,203 --> 01:01:34,620
we've already seen
how we might be

1185
01:01:34,620 --> 01:01:38,220
able to do Q-functions
with deep neural networks.

1186
01:01:38,220 --> 01:01:41,190
But the big problem here
is the count-based bonus.

1187
01:01:41,190 --> 01:01:42,850
We have an infinite
number of states.

1188
01:01:42,850 --> 01:01:44,850
If you think about Atari
or something like that,

1189
01:01:44,850 --> 01:01:46,450
you certainly don't
want to count.

1190
01:01:46,450 --> 01:01:50,490
You're mostly only going to
see one Atari screen once ever.

1191
01:01:50,490 --> 01:01:53,520
And so, these sort of
count-based bonuses

1192
01:01:53,520 --> 01:01:55,740
aren't very realistic.

1193
01:01:55,740 --> 01:01:57,640
And so we're going to
need ways, essentially.

1194
01:01:57,640 --> 01:01:59,630
But why do we have that
count-based bonuses?

1195
01:01:59,630 --> 01:02:01,510
We have the count-based
bonuses to try

1196
01:02:01,510 --> 01:02:04,540
to quantify our uncertainty over
how well do we know the reward

1197
01:02:04,540 --> 01:02:07,030
model for this particular
state in action,

1198
01:02:07,030 --> 01:02:09,160
and how well do we
know the dynamics.

1199
01:02:09,160 --> 01:02:12,618
And so, one of the ideas
when deep RL came around

1200
01:02:12,618 --> 01:02:14,410
was to think about,
could we lift this idea

1201
01:02:14,410 --> 01:02:17,260
and try to quantify our
uncertainty in the deep RL

1202
01:02:17,260 --> 01:02:18,880
setting?

1203
01:02:18,880 --> 01:02:20,980
So we're going to need
to move beyond having

1204
01:02:20,980 --> 01:02:22,660
these very simple
counts to think

1205
01:02:22,660 --> 01:02:26,568
about something that's a higher
level representation of that.

1206
01:02:26,568 --> 01:02:29,110
Now, if we could get that-- and
I haven't told you how we can

1207
01:02:29,110 --> 01:02:29,830
get it yet--

1208
01:02:29,830 --> 01:02:32,560
you could imagine that a lot of
the algorithms we've seen before

1209
01:02:32,560 --> 01:02:34,610
could be extended fairly easily.

1210
01:02:34,610 --> 01:02:38,440
So in particular, if you think
about something like function

1211
01:02:38,440 --> 01:02:41,650
approximation with
Q-learning, we

1212
01:02:41,650 --> 01:02:44,730
could imagine just adding some
sort of bonus term in here.

1213
01:02:44,730 --> 01:02:48,250
So instead of having our
empirical reward plus gamma

1214
01:02:48,250 --> 01:02:51,790
times our target, like
our observed next state

1215
01:02:51,790 --> 01:02:54,470
in action with some
parameter weight,

1216
01:02:54,470 --> 01:02:56,710
we could just plug
in some bonus.

1217
01:02:56,710 --> 01:02:59,420
That's kind of what
MBIE-EB is already doing.

1218
01:02:59,420 --> 01:03:02,890
It's just that our bonus before
was determined by our counts.

1219
01:03:02,890 --> 01:03:04,630
And now, we need some
other way to lift

1220
01:03:04,630 --> 01:03:07,720
that so we can do that for
much more general settings.

1221
01:03:07,720 --> 01:03:12,700
But once we have that, we can
imagine plugging it in here.

1222
01:03:12,700 --> 01:03:14,680
So there's a lot of
different approaches

1223
01:03:14,680 --> 01:03:17,350
that have been developed to
try to think about something

1224
01:03:17,350 --> 01:03:21,940
of density, or quantification
of how many visits we have

1225
01:03:21,940 --> 01:03:24,130
or how much certainty we
have over different parts

1226
01:03:24,130 --> 01:03:26,110
of the state and action space.

1227
01:03:26,110 --> 01:03:28,850
So one of the things that
Marc Bellemare and others did,

1228
01:03:28,850 --> 01:03:30,640
which was pretty
successful, is they

1229
01:03:30,640 --> 01:03:34,810
tried to build pseudo
counts over parts

1230
01:03:34,810 --> 01:03:36,380
of the state and action space.

1231
01:03:36,380 --> 01:03:39,130
So you could imagine maybe
even some particular rooms

1232
01:03:39,130 --> 01:03:41,180
in a video game
many, many times.

1233
01:03:41,180 --> 01:03:43,480
And so, you try to essentially
reduce your uncertainty

1234
01:03:43,480 --> 01:03:45,140
over those.

1235
01:03:45,140 --> 01:03:47,180
There's all sorts
of important details

1236
01:03:47,180 --> 01:03:49,700
here around whether
you-- normally,

1237
01:03:49,700 --> 01:03:54,140
in MBIE-EB, every round you
update all of those counts.

1238
01:03:54,140 --> 01:03:57,150
In reality, if you think
back to deep Q-learning,

1239
01:03:57,150 --> 01:04:01,140
we maintained a buffer of state
action rewards next states.

1240
01:04:01,140 --> 01:04:04,830
Now, you would need to include
those bonus terms in there too.

1241
01:04:04,830 --> 01:04:06,800
And if those bonus terms
are changing, how much

1242
01:04:06,800 --> 01:04:08,040
do you update your buffer?

1243
01:04:08,040 --> 01:04:10,457
Just to give you a sense of
some of the different wrinkles

1244
01:04:10,457 --> 01:04:12,402
one has to think about.

1245
01:04:12,402 --> 01:04:13,910
But the high level
important thing

1246
01:04:13,910 --> 01:04:15,250
is that this matters a lot.

1247
01:04:15,250 --> 01:04:17,870
So in Montezuma's Revenge, which
was early on considered one

1248
01:04:17,870 --> 01:04:21,170
of the hardest Atari
games-- probably still is--

1249
01:04:21,170 --> 01:04:25,500
if you did a standard DQN for 50
million frames, which is a lot,

1250
01:04:25,500 --> 01:04:28,610
it never got past
the second room.

1251
01:04:28,610 --> 01:04:32,210
With epsilon greedy exploration,
it was not strategic.

1252
01:04:32,210 --> 01:04:34,810
It just got very
bad performance.

1253
01:04:34,810 --> 01:04:36,740
But what Marc Bellemare
and others showed

1254
01:04:36,740 --> 01:04:41,270
is that by incorporating a
notion of count-based lifted

1255
01:04:41,270 --> 01:04:46,140
to the generalization case, you
could do far, far, far better.

1256
01:04:46,140 --> 01:04:47,730
So that's just to
highlight that there

1257
01:04:47,730 --> 01:04:51,073
are ways to lift up this
notion of optimism uncertainty

1258
01:04:51,073 --> 01:04:52,115
for this type of setting.

1259
01:04:54,130 --> 01:04:57,020
There is similarly ways
to lift Thompson sampling.

1260
01:04:57,020 --> 01:04:59,590
So, we've done some
work there where

1261
01:04:59,590 --> 01:05:04,240
we think about particular
representations and parameters.

1262
01:05:04,240 --> 01:05:07,150
Ian Osband, who
introduced PSRL, then

1263
01:05:07,150 --> 01:05:09,740
tried to lift it up to
the deep Q-learning case.

1264
01:05:09,740 --> 01:05:12,190
They did it where they were
just bootstrapping samples

1265
01:05:12,190 --> 01:05:15,430
as an approximation.

1266
01:05:15,430 --> 01:05:22,740
That is a pretty coarse
approximation of uncertainty.

1267
01:05:22,740 --> 01:05:25,133
Something else that often
worked pretty well--

1268
01:05:25,133 --> 01:05:26,925
surprisingly well,
given how simple it is--

1269
01:05:26,925 --> 01:05:29,550
is essentially to do something
just at the last layer.

1270
01:05:29,550 --> 01:05:32,730
So the last layer do something
like Bayesian linear regression

1271
01:05:32,730 --> 01:05:35,310
to try to get an uncertainty
estimate, and then

1272
01:05:35,310 --> 01:05:36,430
sample from that.

1273
01:05:36,430 --> 01:05:39,990
So this is a pretty simple
thing one could try.

1274
01:05:39,990 --> 01:05:42,240
There's a lot of
work to do this.

1275
01:05:42,240 --> 01:05:45,600
Let's go back to thinking of
other really recent approaches

1276
01:05:45,600 --> 01:05:49,060
which try to think about doing
this not just for one task,

1277
01:05:49,060 --> 01:05:51,830
but many tasks where you
need to do generalization.

1278
01:05:51,830 --> 01:05:54,960
So early in this lecture, I
introduced the DREAM algorithm

1279
01:05:54,960 --> 01:05:59,640
to you, which we later
used to actually go grading

1280
01:05:59,640 --> 01:06:01,060
of the Breakout assignment.

1281
01:06:01,060 --> 01:06:03,960
The notion in DREAM is that
you have many different tasks

1282
01:06:03,960 --> 01:06:07,815
and you're going to learn how
to explore in them efficiently.

1283
01:06:07,815 --> 01:06:10,620
So that was one example where
we're now really thinking

1284
01:06:10,620 --> 01:06:12,690
about how do we develop
efficient exploration

1285
01:06:12,690 --> 01:06:15,570
strategies by leveraging
structure over the tasks,

1286
01:06:15,570 --> 01:06:18,540
where an agent is going
to do a series of tasks.

1287
01:06:18,540 --> 01:06:20,560
Similarly, in some
of our recent work,

1288
01:06:20,560 --> 01:06:24,160
we introduced decision
pre-trained transformers.

1289
01:06:24,160 --> 01:06:26,260
This was, again, a
meta-learning case.

1290
01:06:26,260 --> 01:06:27,760
The idea is that
your agent is going

1291
01:06:27,760 --> 01:06:29,290
to do a series of
bandit problems,

1292
01:06:29,290 --> 01:06:31,540
or a series of RL
problems, and we

1293
01:06:31,540 --> 01:06:35,440
want to learn how to optimally
explore in those settings.

1294
01:06:35,440 --> 01:06:39,200
So I'll just show you
briefly how it works.

1295
01:06:39,200 --> 01:06:40,720
The idea in this
setting is we're

1296
01:06:40,720 --> 01:06:43,210
going to use a
pre-trained transformer.

1297
01:06:43,210 --> 01:06:46,540
One of the interesting things is
you map reinforcement learning

1298
01:06:46,540 --> 01:06:49,400
to supervised learning,
similar to behavior cloning.

1299
01:06:49,400 --> 01:06:52,160
But instead of relying on the
data you collected in the past,

1300
01:06:52,160 --> 01:06:54,670
if you can compute what would
have been the right action

1301
01:06:54,670 --> 01:06:59,350
to take there, you can train it
to predict that optimal action.

1302
01:06:59,350 --> 01:07:01,610
It turns out that
when you do that,

1303
01:07:01,610 --> 01:07:05,200
we can exactly map that back to
doing the equivalent of Thompson

1304
01:07:05,200 --> 01:07:06,170
sampling.

1305
01:07:06,170 --> 01:07:08,800
So in all the settings for
which Thompson sampling has

1306
01:07:08,800 --> 01:07:12,520
theoretical guarantees, this
decision pre-trained transformer

1307
01:07:12,520 --> 01:07:15,940
can inherit those guarantees,
which is pretty cool.

1308
01:07:15,940 --> 01:07:18,250
The nice thing too is
that, empirically, it

1309
01:07:18,250 --> 01:07:20,950
can allow you to take
advantage of structure that

1310
01:07:20,950 --> 01:07:24,280
is present in your domain
that you didn't have to code.

1311
01:07:24,280 --> 01:07:26,270
So let me just give
you an example of that.

1312
01:07:26,270 --> 01:07:28,480
So what I showed you
earlier in this lecture

1313
01:07:28,480 --> 01:07:30,820
is that if you
have a domain where

1314
01:07:30,820 --> 01:07:35,530
you have some linear
structure, if you give

1315
01:07:35,530 --> 01:07:38,463
that linear structure
to your algorithm, then

1316
01:07:38,463 --> 01:07:39,380
you can do quite well.

1317
01:07:39,380 --> 01:07:40,490
So that's the green line here.

1318
01:07:40,490 --> 01:07:42,260
So this is the amount of
data you have over time.

1319
01:07:42,260 --> 01:07:43,718
And this is your
cumulative regret.

1320
01:07:43,718 --> 01:07:44,660
Lower is better.

1321
01:07:44,660 --> 01:07:47,320
So, most historical
algorithms have

1322
01:07:47,320 --> 01:07:49,760
assumed you give that
structure to your bandit.

1323
01:07:49,760 --> 01:07:52,763
You write down, there
are these 300 features

1324
01:07:52,763 --> 01:07:55,180
that you need to pay attention
to news articles and people

1325
01:07:55,180 --> 01:07:57,040
to figure out what
the reward will be.

1326
01:07:57,040 --> 01:07:59,660
If you give it that structure
and that structure is right,

1327
01:07:59,660 --> 01:08:01,210
you often do pretty well.

1328
01:08:01,210 --> 01:08:03,880
You could not leverage
that structure,

1329
01:08:03,880 --> 01:08:06,520
and you would get
something like this.

1330
01:08:06,520 --> 01:08:08,720
So this is a Thompson
sampling algorithm,

1331
01:08:08,720 --> 01:08:10,780
which just assumes
that it doesn't

1332
01:08:10,780 --> 01:08:12,670
have that linear structure.

1333
01:08:12,670 --> 01:08:14,410
What are the cool
things that we found

1334
01:08:14,410 --> 01:08:17,569
by this approach is
that in this setting,

1335
01:08:17,569 --> 01:08:21,378
if you really have a linear
structure in your domain,

1336
01:08:21,378 --> 01:08:23,170
and you're doing many
tasks and all of them

1337
01:08:23,170 --> 01:08:25,450
have this linear structure,
what are decision

1338
01:08:25,450 --> 01:08:27,069
pre-trained
transformer will learn

1339
01:08:27,069 --> 01:08:29,090
is that even though
you're not telling it,

1340
01:08:29,090 --> 01:08:32,890
it will realize it can more
compactly encode that structure.

1341
01:08:32,890 --> 01:08:35,870
And so, when you deploy
it on a new task,

1342
01:08:35,870 --> 01:08:38,529
you will get behavior
almost as if you gave it

1343
01:08:38,529 --> 01:08:40,899
the unknown structure.

1344
01:08:40,899 --> 01:08:42,609
So I think this is
really interesting

1345
01:08:42,609 --> 01:08:45,220
because often one of the brittle
aspects of machine learning

1346
01:08:45,220 --> 01:08:48,122
is that we originally wrote down
these sort of representations.

1347
01:08:48,122 --> 01:08:50,830
And of course, one of the really
amazing things for deep learning

1348
01:08:50,830 --> 01:08:52,510
is that we're trying
to not write down

1349
01:08:52,510 --> 01:08:54,399
specific
representations as much,

1350
01:08:54,399 --> 01:08:57,200
and get much closer
to the input raw data.

1351
01:08:57,200 --> 01:09:00,310
And this is illustrating that
in terms of sequential decision

1352
01:09:00,310 --> 01:09:03,319
making and meta exploration
for multiple tasks,

1353
01:09:03,319 --> 01:09:07,359
we can do something similar
here, where we can inductively

1354
01:09:07,359 --> 01:09:11,380
learn that that's a more compact
way to represent the domains,

1355
01:09:11,380 --> 01:09:17,200
and get this much more efficient
exploration in new tasks.

1356
01:09:17,200 --> 01:09:17,700
All right.

1357
01:09:17,700 --> 01:09:20,720
So just to conclude,
we're wrapping up

1358
01:09:20,720 --> 01:09:23,800
our notion of data efficient
reinforcement learning today.

1359
01:09:23,800 --> 01:09:25,670
You should understand
this tension

1360
01:09:25,670 --> 01:09:27,620
between exploration
and exploitation

1361
01:09:27,620 --> 01:09:28,979
in reinforcement learning.

1362
01:09:28,979 --> 01:09:30,109
I haven't used these words.

1363
01:09:30,109 --> 01:09:31,109
They're not great words.

1364
01:09:31,109 --> 01:09:32,500
So I don't use these.

1365
01:09:32,500 --> 01:09:33,750
I haven't used a lot in there.

1366
01:09:33,750 --> 01:09:35,840
But exploration, meaning
you're taking time

1367
01:09:35,840 --> 01:09:37,932
to learn about the
domain and exploitation,

1368
01:09:37,932 --> 01:09:39,890
meaning that you're
leveraging that information

1369
01:09:39,890 --> 01:09:42,529
to make good decisions in
the context of reinforcement

1370
01:09:42,529 --> 01:09:43,670
learning.

1371
01:09:43,670 --> 01:09:45,439
You should be able
to define and compare

1372
01:09:45,439 --> 01:09:48,510
different notions of good,
whether empirical, convergence,

1373
01:09:48,510 --> 01:09:50,503
regret, and PAC.

1374
01:09:50,503 --> 01:09:51,920
You should know
for the algorithms

1375
01:09:51,920 --> 01:09:54,270
we've talked about,
do they have--

1376
01:09:54,270 --> 01:09:58,170
for example, is greedy
sublinear regret?

1377
01:09:58,170 --> 01:09:59,480
Which it's not.

1378
01:09:59,480 --> 01:10:01,640
You should understand
the proof sketch

1379
01:10:01,640 --> 01:10:05,440
I did of why upper confidence
bound is sublinear in regret.

1380
01:10:05,440 --> 01:10:05,940
All right.

1381
01:10:05,940 --> 01:10:10,010
And then next week, we're going
to talk about AlphaGo and how

1382
01:10:10,010 --> 01:10:12,020
do we think about doing
SMART adaptive tree

1383
01:10:12,020 --> 01:10:13,740
search in really large games.

1384
01:10:13,740 --> 01:10:15,610
See you then.

